      authorName: 'Caroll Montague Price',
      title: 'Acceptance Was a Turning Point',
      content: 'For a long time, Caroll believed that getting better meant winning a daily argument with herself.\n\nOn good mornings, she could imagine a future that felt possible. On difficult mornings, the same future seemed to move several steps farther away. The harder she tried to force herself into feeling better, the more exhausted she became.\n\nThere was a particular kind of frustration that came from knowing what you wanted and still struggling to reach it. Other people could see small improvements. Inside, however, the unfinished work felt enormous.\n\nShe began to notice how often her mind used words like “should.”\n\nI should be stronger.\n\nI should be over this.\n\nI should not need help.\n\nEvery sentence sounded like motivation, but together they became another source of pressure.\n\nOne evening, instead of fighting the feeling, she sat with it.\n\nShe did not tell herself that everything was fine.\n\nShe did not pretend the pain was useful.\n\nShe simply admitted the truth: this was hard.\n\nThat sentence felt strangely different from failure.\n\nIt was honest.\n\nAnd honesty gave her something she had not had for a while—room to think.\n\nMaybe accepting that something was difficult did not mean surrendering to it.\n\nMaybe acceptance could be the beginning of deciding what to do next.\n\nFor the first time, she wondered what would happen if she stopped measuring recovery by how quickly the pain disappeared.',
      category: 'Recovery',
