      chapters: [
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 1 — The Fight Against Yourself',
          content: 'For a long time, Caroll believed that getting better meant winning a daily argument with herself.\n\nOn good mornings, she could imagine a future that felt possible. On difficult mornings, the same future seemed to move several steps farther away. The harder she tried to force herself into feeling better, the more exhausted she became.\n\nThere was a particular kind of frustration that came from knowing what you wanted and still struggling to reach it. Other people could see small improvements. Inside, however, the unfinished work felt enormous.\n\nShe began to notice how often her mind used words like “should.”\n\nI should be stronger.\n\nI should be over this.\n\nI should not need help.\n\nEvery sentence sounded like motivation, but together they became another source of pressure.\n\nOne evening, instead of fighting the feeling, she sat with it.\n\nShe did not tell herself that everything was fine.\n\nShe did not pretend the pain was useful.\n\nShe simply admitted the truth: this was hard.\n\nThat sentence felt strangely different from failure.\n\nIt was honest.\n\nAnd honesty gave her something she had not had for a while—room to think.\n\nMaybe accepting that something was difficult did not mean surrendering to it.\n\nMaybe acceptance could be the beginning of deciding what to do next.\n\nFor the first time, she wondered what would happen if she stopped measuring recovery by how quickly the pain disappeared.',
          cliffhanger: 'What happened next would force Caroll to discover the difference between accepting a difficult reality and giving up on changing it.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 2 — The Space to Begin Again',
          content: 'Acceptance did not arrive as one dramatic moment.\n\nIt appeared in small decisions.\n\nCaroll began separating two ideas that had become tangled together: “This is happening” and “This will always happen.”\n\nThe first was a fact about the present.\n\nThe second was a prediction.\n\nShe had been treating predictions as facts.\n\nOnce she noticed that, she started paying attention to the language in her head. When a difficult day appeared, she practiced describing the day instead of defining herself by it.\n\nToday is difficult.\n\nI am struggling today.\n\nI need support today.\n\nThose sentences were not as frightening as “I am broken.”\n\nShe also became more patient with progress. Some days brought movement. Others brought very little. She learned that recovery could be uneven without being meaningless.\n\nSupport became part of the process rather than proof that she had failed.\n\nShe listened more carefully to people who cared about her. She asked questions. She learned to recognize that receiving help did not erase independence.\n\nThere was still fear.\n\nWhat if tomorrow was worse?\n\nWhat if progress disappeared?\n\nWhat if she had misunderstood the whole process?\n\nBut each time those questions appeared, she returned to what she actually knew.\n\nShe was here.\n\nShe was learning.\n\nShe had options.\n\nThat was enough for one day.\n\nThen a setback arrived.\n\nIt was not huge at first.\n\nA difficult thought.\n\nA familiar emotion.\n\nA morning when getting out of bed felt harder than expected.\n\nCaroll felt the old panic rise.\n\nHad all that progress been an illusion?',
          cliffhanger: 'The setback looked exactly like the beginning—and for a moment, Caroll could not tell whether she had moved forward at all.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 3 — When Progress Disappears From View',
          content: 'The setback frightened her because it challenged the story she had been telling herself about recovery.\n\nShe had imagined progress as a staircase.\n\nStep one.\n\nStep two.\n\nStep three.\n\nNever backward.\n\nReal life was different.\n\nSome days felt like steps forward. Some felt like standing still. A few felt like slipping.\n\nBut standing still was not the same as returning to the beginning.\n\nCaroll started looking at progress differently.\n\nShe remembered things she could do now that had once felt impossible. She remembered conversations she could have now. She remembered moments when she recognized a warning sign earlier than before.\n\nThe setback had not erased those skills.\n\nIt had tested them.\n\nThat distinction mattered.\n\nInstead of asking, “Why am I back here?” she asked, “What is this moment teaching me?”\n\nThe question changed the tone of the day.\n\nShe looked for patterns.\n\nWas she exhausted?\n\nHad she isolated herself?\n\nHad she ignored something she needed?\n\nHad she stopped using the support that normally helped?\n\nThere was no single answer.\n\nThere did not need to be.\n\nA map does not need to explain every road to be useful.\n\nSlowly, the setback became information.\n\nAnd information could be used.\n\nCaroll realized that acceptance was not simply accepting pain. It was accepting the truth of where she was standing so she could choose where to go next.\n\nThat night, she wrote down one sentence:\n\n“I can accept today without giving up on tomorrow.”\n\nShe read it again.\n\nThen she closed the notebook.\n\nFor the first time in days, the future felt slightly less like a test and slightly more like an open door.',
          cliffhanger: 'But the next morning brought a choice: return to the old pressure—or build recovery around patience instead.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 4 — A Different Definition of Strength',
          content: 'Caroll began to understand that strength was not always visible.\n\nSometimes strength looked like continuing treatment.\n\nSometimes it looked like admitting something was not working.\n\nSometimes it looked like resting before exhaustion became a crisis.\n\nSometimes it looked like accepting kindness.\n\nShe stopped expecting herself to become fearless.\n\nInstead, she wanted to become more prepared.\n\nA difficult emotion could arrive without becoming an emergency.\n\nA setback could happen without becoming a verdict.\n\nA bad week could exist without defining an entire year.\n\nThis did not make recovery easy.\n\nIt made recovery more realistic.\n\nShe discovered that patience was not waiting passively for life to improve. It was refusing to punish herself for taking time.\n\nAcceptance gave her that patience.\n\nAnd patience gave her room to continue.\n\nYears later, she could look back and see that the turning point had not been the day everything became easy.\n\nIt was the period when she stopped demanding perfection before allowing herself to move forward.\n\nThe lesson stayed simple:\n\nYou can acknowledge where you are without deciding that you must stay there.\n\nYou can accept help without becoming weak.\n\nYou can have a difficult chapter without calling the whole book a failure.\n\nCaroll\'s recovery became less about defeating herself and more about learning how to stand beside herself.\n\nAnd that changed the question she asked on difficult days.\n\nNot “Why can\'t I be better already?”\n\nBut:\n\n“What is one kind thing I can do for myself today?”\n\nThat question did not solve everything.\n\nIt gave her something more useful.\n\nA next step.',
          cliffhanger: 'Sometimes the smallest next step is the one that changes the entire direction of the story.',
        ),
      ],
    ),

    StoryModel(
      id: 'featured_jonathan_recovery_try',
      authorUid: 'trusted-featured',
      authorName: 'Jonathan Anon',
      title: 'Recovery Can Take More Than One Try',
      content: 'Jonathan once believed that asking for help meant he had finally reached the point where everything would change.\n\nHe wanted a clean beginning.\n\nA plan.\n\nA finish line.\n\nThe idea was comforting: start recovery, follow the path, become well, and leave the difficult period behind.\n\nBut real recovery rarely follows a neat schedule.\n\nThe first attempt taught him that wanting change and being ready for every part of change were not the same thing.\n\nThere were days when he felt determined.\n\nThere were days when old habits returned.\n\nThere were moments when progress seemed obvious, followed by moments when it seemed to disappear.\n\nEach setback felt personal.\n\nInstead of seeing it as information, Jonathan saw it as evidence.\n\nMaybe I cannot do this.\n\nMaybe I am not serious enough.\n\nMaybe I have already failed.\n\nThose thoughts became heavier than the original problem.\n\nEventually, he had to confront an uncomfortable truth: the first attempt had not given him the ending he wanted.\n\nBut it had given him something else.\n\nExperience.\n\nHe knew more about his triggers.\n\nHe knew what support felt helpful.\n\nHe knew which warning signs he had ignored.\n\nHe knew that pretending to be fine was not the same as being fine.\n\nHe did not realize how important that knowledge would become.\n\nBecause soon, he would need to start again.',
