      chapters: [
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 1 — The Weight Nobody Could See',
          content: 'Destiny learned that loneliness was not always about being physically alone.\n\nA person could sit in a crowded room and still feel unreachable.\n\nShe could answer messages, attend ordinary events, smile at people, and still carry a private weight that nobody knew how to name.\n\nFor a while, she thought hiding that weight was a form of strength.\n\nIf nobody knew, nobody could misunderstand.\n\nIf nobody knew, nobody could judge.\n\nIf nobody knew, she would not have to explain.\n\nBut hiding pain has a cost.\n\nIt makes every difficult thought echo louder.\n\nOne day, Destiny noticed that she was spending more energy pretending she was okay than actually taking care of herself.\n\nThat realization scared her.\n\nWhat would happen if she told someone the truth?\n\nShe chose one person.\n\nNot everyone.\n\nNot a room full of people.\n\nOne person she trusted.\n\nThe conversation was awkward.\n\nThere were pauses.\n\nThere were sentences she could not finish.\n\nBut the other person did not demand a perfect explanation.\n\nThey listened.\n\nThat was the beginning.\n\nNot a cure.\n\nNot a miracle.\n\nA beginning.\n\nDestiny discovered that being understood did not require another person to experience exactly what she experienced.\n\nSometimes understanding simply meant someone was willing to stay.',
          cliffhanger: 'But one honest conversation opened a door—and Destiny was about to discover what was on the other side.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 2 — A Community Is Built Slowly',
          content: 'Support did not appear all at once.\n\nIt grew through repeated moments.\n\nA message asking how she was doing.\n\nA person remembering something important.\n\nA conversation where she did not have to perform happiness.\n\nA space where she could say, “Today is difficult,” without immediately apologizing.\n\nDestiny began to understand the difference between being around people and feeling connected to them.\n\nConnection required safety.\n\nIt required respect.\n\nIt required room for honesty.\n\nShe also learned that compassion was not the same as saying everything was okay.\n\nReal compassion could include difficult truths.\n\nIt could mean encouraging someone to seek professional help.\n\nIt could mean setting a boundary.\n\nIt could mean listening without trying to fix every problem.\n\nAs Destiny became more comfortable receiving support, she became more comfortable offering it.\n\nNot as an expert.\n\nAs a person.\n\nShe knew what it felt like to be afraid of being judged.\n\nSo she listened differently.\n\nShe knew what it felt like to believe that nobody would understand.\n\nSo she stayed present.\n\nSlowly, her circle became more than a list of names.\n\nIt became a network.\n\nNot perfect.\n\nHuman.\n\nThere were misunderstandings.\n\nThere were days when people were unavailable.\n\nThere were moments when she still felt alone.\n\nBut now loneliness was no longer proof that she had nobody.\n\nIt was a signal to reconnect.',
          cliffhanger: 'Then a difficult period arrived when the people she depended on could not be there in the way she expected.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 3 — When Support Feels Far Away',
          content: 'The hardest lesson came when Destiny reached out and did not immediately get the response she hoped for.\n\nOne person was busy.\n\nAnother did not understand.\n\nA message remained unanswered.\n\nThe old conclusion arrived quickly:\n\nSee? You are alone.\n\nFor a moment, she believed it.\n\nThen she remembered what she had learned.\n\nOne unavailable person was not the same as no support.\n\nOne unanswered message was not a verdict on her worth.\n\nShe widened the circle.\n\nShe considered professional support.\n\nShe looked for healthier spaces.\n\nShe gave herself permission to say exactly what she needed instead of hoping someone would guess.\n\nThe process was uncomfortable.\n\nBut it worked.\n\nShe began building layers of support rather than placing all her hope on one relationship.\n\nThat made connection stronger.\n\nIt also made her more independent, not less.\n\nBecause independence was no longer “I need nobody.”\n\nIt became “I know how to find support when I need it.”\n\nThat distinction changed how she saw herself.\n\nShe was not weak for needing people.\n\nHumans need people.\n\nThe next time loneliness appeared, Destiny recognized it earlier.\n\nShe did not wait until the feeling became unbearable.\n\nShe sent a message.\n\nShe stepped into a supportive space.\n\nShe talked.\n\nAnd when the urge to withdraw returned, she remembered the first night she had told someone the truth.\n\nThat tiny act had changed the direction of her recovery.\n\nShe could do something tiny again.',
          cliffhanger: 'The next decision was simple on paper—but emotionally, it would ask Destiny to trust connection one more time.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 4 — Let Yourself Be Found',
          content: 'Destiny eventually learned that community is not something you find once.\n\nIt is something you build.\n\nYou build it through honesty.\n\nThrough boundaries.\n\nThrough asking.\n\nThrough listening.\n\nThrough returning.\n\nAnd sometimes, through trying again after disappointment.\n\nCompassion became part of that process.\n\nNot only compassion from others.\n\nCompassion toward herself.\n\nShe stopped asking why she could not handle everything alone.\n\nShe started asking what kind of support would make the next step easier.\n\nThat question gave her choices.\n\nSometimes she needed a conversation.\n\nSometimes professional help.\n\nSometimes quiet.\n\nSometimes a reminder that she was not the only person struggling.\n\nThe important thing was that she no longer treated isolation as her default answer.\n\nShe had learned that a person can be strong and still need a hand.\n\nA person can be independent and still want company.\n\nA person can be recovering and still have difficult days.\n\nNone of those truths cancel the others.\n\nYears later, Destiny could trace an important line back to that first honest conversation.\n\nShe had not solved everything that day.\n\nShe had simply allowed herself to be seen.\n\nThat was enough to begin.\n\nAnd perhaps that is what connection sometimes is.\n\nNot someone rescuing you.\n\nSomeone reminding you that you do not have to carry every part of the journey alone.',
          cliffhanger: 'The next time loneliness arrives, remember the first small door: tell one safe person the truth.',
