      title: 'Finding My Way Back to Self-Love',
      content: 'Wyatt had learned to be hard on himself.\n\nAt first, the criticism sounded useful.\n\nDo better.\n\nTry harder.\n\nStop making excuses.\n\nBut over time, the voice became less like a coach and more like an enemy.\n\nEvery mistake became evidence.\n\nEvery slow day became a character flaw.\n\nEvery difficult emotion became something to hide.\n\nThe problem with living under constant criticism is that eventually you stop noticing how loud it is.\n\nYou simply believe it.\n\nWyatt began to wonder whether self-love was even possible for someone who had spent so long distrusting himself.\n\nThe idea felt almost embarrassing.\n\nHe could understand supporting another person.\n\nHe could understand forgiving a friend.\n\nBut offering the same patience to himself felt unnatural.\n\nOne evening, he caught himself thinking, “I don\'t deserve a break.”\n\nHe stopped.\n\nWho had taught him that?\n\nWhy did rest have to be earned?\n\nWhy did care have to wait until he became perfect?\n\nHe did not have answers.\n\nBut the questions mattered.\n\nHe decided to experiment.\n\nFor one day, he would speak to himself as he would speak to someone he cared about.\n\nNot positive every minute.\n\nJust fair.\n\nThe result was uncomfortable.\n\nAnd strangely relieving.',
