        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 2 — Learning to Be on Your Own Side',
          content: 'Self-love did not begin with loving everything about himself.\n\nIt began with stopping the constant punishment.\n\nWhen Wyatt made a mistake, he practiced describing it without attacking himself.\n\nI made a mistake.\n\nI need to repair it.\n\nI can learn from it.\n\nThat was different from:\n\nI always ruin everything.\n\nThe second sentence offered no path forward.\n\nThe first did.\n\nHe also started noticing what made the inner criticism louder.\n\nExhaustion.\n\nIsolation.\n\nComparing himself with other people.\n\nExpecting immediate results.\n\nThe patterns were not excuses.\n\nThey were information.\n\nOnce he saw them, he could prepare.\n\nHe learned to protect sleep.\n\nHe made space for supportive relationships.\n\nHe celebrated small progress instead of waiting for dramatic transformation.\n\nSome days, he still hated how slowly things moved.\n\nBut patience became a skill.\n\nSelf-love was not a feeling he had to wait for.\n\nIt was behavior.\n\nEating when hungry.\n\nResting when exhausted.\n\nAsking for help when overwhelmed.\n\nKeeping promises to himself.\n\nWalking away from situations that repeatedly harmed his peace.\n\nThe more he practiced these things, the less foreign self-respect felt.\n\nThen a difficult memory returned.\n\nThe old voice came back with it.\n\n“You haven\'t changed.”\n\nWyatt believed it for a moment.\n\nThen he opened an old journal and found a sentence he had written months earlier.\n\nHe was surprised by how different the person on the page sounded.',
          cliffhanger: 'The old journal contained proof that Wyatt\'s memory was lying to him—and the next page showed how far he had actually come.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 3 — Proof Hidden in Small Things',
          content: 'Wyatt read the old pages slowly.\n\nThere were no grand achievements.\n\nNo perfect transformation.\n\nInstead, there were small records.\n\nA day he had asked for help.\n\nA day he had taken a break instead of pushing until exhaustion.\n\nA difficult conversation he had survived.\n\nA moment when he had chosen not to disappear.\n\nAt the time, each event had felt insignificant.\n\nTogether, they formed evidence.\n\nHe had changed.\n\nNot into someone who never struggled.\n\nInto someone who responded differently.\n\nThat distinction became powerful.\n\nSelf-love did not mean believing you were amazing every morning.\n\nSometimes it meant refusing to use pain as a weapon against yourself.\n\nSometimes it meant saying, “This is hard, and I still deserve care.”\n\nWyatt began to understand that self-worth was not a reward for perfect behavior.\n\nIt was a starting point.\n\nYou do not have to become worthy.\n\nYou have to stop treating yourself as though you are unworthy.\n\nThe idea followed him into relationships.\n\nHe became more willing to communicate.\n\nMore willing to set boundaries.\n\nMore willing to leave conversations that became cruel.\n\nNot because he believed he was better than anyone.\n\nBecause he was learning that respect had to work both ways.\n\nOne evening, he stood in front of a mirror.\n\nThe face looking back was not magically transformed.\n\nBut the person behind it was different.\n\nHe did not need to love every part of himself tonight.\n\nHe only needed to stop abandoning himself.',
          cliffhanger: 'Then Wyatt faced the hardest test of self-love: what would happen when he made a mistake again?',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 4 — Staying With Yourself',
          content: 'The mistake came.\n\nIt was ordinary.\n\nThat almost made it worse.\n\nWyatt immediately felt the old urge to punish himself.\n\nHe wanted to replay it.\n\nAnalyze it.\n\nTurn it into a statement about who he was.\n\nInstead, he paused.\n\nHe remembered the journal.\n\nHe remembered the small changes.\n\nHe remembered that growth did not create immunity from mistakes.\n\nSo he repaired what he could.\n\nHe apologized where necessary.\n\nHe learned what he could.\n\nThen he stopped.\n\nHe did not spend the rest of the day attacking himself.\n\nThat was the victory.\n\nNot that the mistake disappeared.\n\nThat he stayed on his own side afterward.\n\nSelf-love had become practical.\n\nIt was no longer a slogan.\n\nIt was a way of responding.\n\nWhen life was hard, he did not ask, “How can I punish myself into becoming better?”\n\nHe asked, “What would help me become better without destroying me in the process?”\n\nThe answer changed from day to day.\n\nSometimes support.\n\nSometimes rest.\n\nSometimes accountability.\n\nSometimes professional guidance.\n\nSometimes simply beginning again.\n\nWyatt eventually understood that recovery and self-love were connected.\n\nWhen you believe your life has value, you become more willing to protect it.\n\nYou seek support sooner.\n\nYou notice warning signs.\n\nYou choose relationships that respect you.\n\nYou allow your future to matter.\n\nAnd on difficult days, you remember that a painful chapter is still only a chapter.',
          cliffhanger: 'The next time your inner voice becomes cruel, ask one question: would you speak to someone you love this way?',
        ),
      ],
    ),

    StoryModel(
      id: 'featured_max_support',
      authorUid: 'trusted-featured',
      authorName: 'Max E. Guttman, LCSW',
      title: 'Let Someone Stand Beside You',
      content: 'Max learned an important lesson about recovery from the people who remained close during difficult periods.\n\nSupport can be easy to appreciate when everything is going well.\n\nIt becomes much more meaningful when life becomes complicated.\n\nThere were moments when words were difficult.\n\nMoments when explaining what was happening felt exhausting.\n\nMoments when accepting help felt like admitting defeat.\n\nBut the people beside him did something simple.\n\nThey stayed.\n\nThey did not need to understand every detail immediately.\n\nThey did not need to solve every problem.\n\nTheir presence created a different kind of safety.\n\nMax began noticing how much recovery is affected by the environment around a person.\n\nA supportive voice can interrupt a spiral.\n\nA calm conversation can create perspective.\n\nA reminder to eat, rest, attend an appointment, or simply step outside can matter on a difficult day.\n\nThese things may look small from the outside.\n\nInside a hard moment, they can be enormous.\n\nMax also learned that support is not one-size-fits-all.\n\nSometimes you need advice.\n\nSometimes you need someone to listen.\n\nSometimes you need someone to tell you the truth.\n\nSometimes you simply need someone to sit beside you.\n\nThe challenge was learning to say which one you needed.',
      category: 'Life',
      coverEmoji: '🫶',
      isFeatured: true,
      sourceName: 'NAMI Personal Stories',
      sourceUrl: 'https://www.nami.org/personal-stories/my-parents-support-shaped-my-recovery/',
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 1, 1),
      tags: [
        'support',
        'family',
        'connection',
        'recovery',
        'trust',
      ],
      chapters: [
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 1 — The People Who Stayed',
          content: 'Max learned an important lesson about recovery from the people who remained close during difficult periods.\n\nSupport can be easy to appreciate when everything is going well.\n\nIt becomes much more meaningful when life becomes complicated.\n\nThere were moments when words were difficult.\n\nMoments when explaining what was happening felt exhausting.\n\nMoments when accepting help felt like admitting defeat.\n\nBut the people beside him did something simple.\n\nThey stayed.\n\nThey did not need to understand every detail immediately.\n\nThey did not need to solve every problem.\n\nTheir presence created a different kind of safety.\n\nMax began noticing how much recovery is affected by the environment around a person.\n\nA supportive voice can interrupt a spiral.\n\nA calm conversation can create perspective.\n\nA reminder to eat, rest, attend an appointment, or simply step outside can matter on a difficult day.\n\nThese things may look small from the outside.\n\nInside a hard moment, they can be enormous.\n\nMax also learned that support is not one-size-fits-all.\n\nSometimes you need advice.\n\nSometimes you need someone to listen.\n\nSometimes you need someone to tell you the truth.\n\nSometimes you simply need someone to sit beside you.\n\nThe challenge was learning to say which one you needed.',
          cliffhanger: 'But support would soon become complicated by a question many people fear: what happens when the person helping you gets tired too?',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 2 — Support Without Losing Yourself',
          content: 'Max began to understand that healthy support is not dependence.\n\nIt is partnership.\n\nA person in recovery still needs agency.\n\nStill needs choices.\n\nStill needs to learn how to speak for themselves.\n\nThe people around him could encourage, listen, and help.\n\nThey could not live his life for him.\n\nThat balance mattered.\n\nHe started communicating more clearly.\n\nInstead of waiting for people to guess, he tried to say what would help.\n\n“I need you to listen.”\n\n“I need some space.”\n\n“I need help making this decision.”\n\n“I need you to remind me that this feeling will pass.”\n\nThe conversations were not always smooth.\n\nSometimes people misunderstood.\n\nSometimes expectations clashed.\n\nBut honest communication was better than silent resentment.\n\nMax also realized that supporters need care too.\n\nNo single person can be available every hour.\n\nNo family can carry an entire recovery alone.\n\nProfessional support, community resources, trusted friends, and personal coping skills all have a place.\n\nBuilding a wider network made relationships healthier.\n\nIt also reduced the fear that one unavailable person meant everything was falling apart.\n\nThe more stable the support system became, the more confidently Max could move through difficult periods.\n\nThen came a week when several things went wrong at once.\n\nThe people he normally leaned on were busy.\n\nThe familiar safety net felt thinner.\n\nFor a moment, panic returned.',
          cliffhanger: 'This time, Max had to discover whether support meant having one person—or knowing where else to turn.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 3 — Building More Than One Door',
          content: 'Max remembered a lesson that had taken time to understand.\n\nA strong support system has more than one door.\n\nThere can be family.\n\nFriends.\n\nProfessional care.\n\nPeer communities.\n\nTrusted mentors.\n\nPersonal routines.\n\nAnd the ability to recognize when additional help is needed.\n\nHe began writing down his support options.\n\nNot because he expected a crisis.\n\nBecause preparation reduces the pressure of making decisions while overwhelmed.\n\nThe list was surprisingly long.\n\nThat discovery changed his sense of isolation.\n\nHe had been measuring support by who was available at one exact moment.\n\nNow he measured it by the network he had built over time.\n\nThat network was not perfect.\n\nBut it existed.\n\nAnd he knew how to use it.\n\nThe difficult week eventually passed.\n\nAfterward, Max realized something important.\n\nThe people who had supported him had not made him weaker.\n\nThey had helped him learn how to support himself.\n\nTheir care had become skills.\n\nTheir reminders had become routines.\n\nTheir encouragement had become self-belief.\n\nTheir presence had taught him that accepting help and becoming independent can happen at the same time.\n\nRecovery did not require standing alone.\n\nIt required learning how to stand—with the right people nearby when needed.',
          cliffhanger: 'Then Max faced a new challenge: could he become the kind of person who offers the same steady support to someone else?',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 4 — Passing the Light Forward',
          content: 'Over time, Max noticed that the experience of receiving support had changed how he listened to other people.\n\nHe became less interested in fixing everything immediately.\n\nMore interested in understanding.\n\nHe learned that a person in pain may not need a perfect answer.\n\nThey may need a safe conversation.\n\nA little patience.\n\nA reminder that asking for help is allowed.\n\nHe also learned the importance of boundaries.\n\nBeing supportive does not mean carrying someone else\'s entire life.\n\nIt means standing beside them while they take their own steps.\n\nThat became one of the deepest lessons of his recovery.\n\nPeople can be part of your strength without becoming responsible for your survival.\n\nYou can love someone without being their only support.\n\nYou can ask for help without giving away your agency.\n\nAnd you can become stronger partly because someone once stayed beside you.\n\nMax looked back at the difficult periods differently.\n\nThe memories were still difficult.\n\nBut they no longer told only a story of struggle.\n\nThey also told a story of connection.\n\nPeople had stayed.\n\nHe had learned.\n\nHe had asked.\n\nHe had grown.\n\nAnd now he knew something he wished every struggling person could know:\n\nYou do not have to prove that you can carry everything alone.\n\nSometimes strength begins when you finally let someone stand beside you.',
          cliffhanger: 'The next time you feel alone, remember that reaching for one safe connection can be a recovery skill—not a weakness.',
        ),
      ],
    ),

    StoryModel(
      id: 'featured_samhsa_journey',
      authorUid: 'trusted-featured',
      authorName: 'SAMHSA RecoverMe',
      title: 'Recovery Is a Journey, Not a Single Moment',
      content: 'Imagine being told that recovery is possible, but never being told exactly when you will feel recovered.\n\nFor someone desperate for relief, that uncertainty can be frustrating.\n\nA person may want a date.\n\nA number.\n\nA finish line.\n\nBut recovery is often a process rather than a single event.\n\nThat idea can feel disappointing until you begin to see what a process allows.\n\nA process allows change.\n\nLearning.\n\nSetbacks.\n\nSupport.\n\nNew skills.\n\nDifferent directions.\n\nThe first step may be tiny.\n\nGetting out of bed.\n\nMaking an appointment.\n\nTelling someone the truth.\n\nChoosing not to act on an urge for ten minutes.\n\nTrying again after a difficult day.\n\nNone of these looks like a dramatic transformation.\n\nTogether, they can become a path.\n\nThe problem is that people often compare their first steps with someone else\'s middle.\n\nThat comparison creates hopelessness.\n\nSo the journey begins with a different question:\n\nWhat is the next healthy step available to me today?\n\nNot forever.\n\nToday.\n\nThat question makes the road smaller.\n\nAnd smaller roads are easier to walk.',
      category: 'Recovery',
      coverEmoji: '🛤️',
      isFeatured: true,
      sourceName: 'SAMHSA RecoverMe',
      sourceUrl: 'https://www.samhsa.gov/recoverme',
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 1, 1),
      tags: [
        'recovery',
        'journey',
        'hope',
        'support',
        'progress',
      ],
      chapters: [
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 1 — Looking for the Finish Line',
          content: 'Imagine being told that recovery is possible, but never being told exactly when you will feel recovered.\n\nFor someone desperate for relief, that uncertainty can be frustrating.\n\nA person may want a date.\n\nA number.\n\nA finish line.\n\nBut recovery is often a process rather than a single event.\n\nThat idea can feel disappointing until you begin to see what a process allows.\n\nA process allows change.\n\nLearning.\n\nSetbacks.\n\nSupport.\n\nNew skills.\n\nDifferent directions.\n\nThe first step may be tiny.\n\nGetting out of bed.\n\nMaking an appointment.\n\nTelling someone the truth.\n\nChoosing not to act on an urge for ten minutes.\n\nTrying again after a difficult day.\n\nNone of these looks like a dramatic transformation.\n\nTogether, they can become a path.\n\nThe problem is that people often compare their first steps with someone else\'s middle.\n\nThat comparison creates hopelessness.\n\nSo the journey begins with a different question:\n\nWhat is the next healthy step available to me today?\n\nNot forever.\n\nToday.\n\nThat question makes the road smaller.\n\nAnd smaller roads are easier to walk.',
          cliffhanger: 'But every journey reaches a point where the traveler wonders whether the small steps are actually taking them anywhere.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 2 — The Days That Look Like Nothing',
          content: 'Some recovery days look impressive.\n\nOthers look like nothing happened.\n\nA person attends an appointment.\n\nUses a coping strategy.\n\nTalks to someone.\n\nGets through a difficult evening.\n\nThen goes to sleep.\n\nThere may be no celebration.\n\nBut something happened.\n\nA healthier response was practiced.\n\nThe brain and behavior both learn through repetition.\n\nThat means ordinary choices matter.\n\nA person does not have to wait for a dramatic crisis before taking recovery seriously.\n\nEarly warning signs can become opportunities to act sooner.\n\nA difficult mood can become a reason to slow down.\n\nAn urge can become a signal to seek support.\n\nA mistake can become information.\n\nThis way of thinking changes the meaning of progress.\n\nProgress is not only “feeling good.”\n\nIt can be “responding differently.”\n\nOne week may contain three steps forward and two steps back.\n\nThat does not make the three steps imaginary.\n\nThe journey is still moving.\n\nThe traveler simply has to keep adjusting.\n\nThis is where support becomes important.\n\nPeople rarely walk difficult journeys well in complete isolation.\n\nConnection can provide encouragement, accountability, perspective, and hope.\n\nBut support also has to be personal.\n\nWhat helps one person may not help another.\n\nRecovery is not a competition.\n\nIt is a process of discovering what supports your own life.',
          cliffhanger: 'Then one difficult day arrived, and the traveler had to decide whether a setback meant the journey had failed.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 3 — The Detour',
          content: 'The setback felt bigger than it was.\n\nThat is one of the strange things about difficult moments.\n\nThey can make the road behind you disappear.\n\nAll you can see is the obstacle.\n\nBut a detour is not the same as the destination.\n\nA person may return to an old habit.\n\nMiss an appointment.\n\nWithdraw from people.\n\nLose motivation.\n\nFeel overwhelmed.\n\nThe important question is what happens next.\n\nShame often says, “You have failed, so stop trying.”\n\nRecovery asks a different question:\n\n“What support do you need now?”\n\nThat question opens possibilities.\n\nTalk to someone.\n\nReview what happened.\n\nIdentify triggers.\n\nChange the environment.\n\nReturn to treatment.\n\nRest.\n\nBegin again.\n\nThe response after a setback can become part of recovery too.\n\nThis is why a journey metaphor matters.\n\nJourneys contain wrong turns.\n\nThey contain slow sections.\n\nThey contain places where you need directions.\n\nSometimes you even need to stop and recover before continuing.\n\nNone of those things means the road has disappeared.\n\nThe traveler learns.\n\nThe map becomes more detailed.\n\nThe next decision becomes more informed.\n\nEventually, the obstacle passes.\n\nNot because it was meaningless.\n\nBecause it was navigated.\n\nAnd the journey continues.',
          cliffhanger: 'The road ahead was still unknown, but the traveler had learned the most important skill: keep moving toward support, even after a wrong turn.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 4 — There Is More Road Ahead',
          content: 'Recovery can change the way a person imagines the future.\n\nAt first, the future may be nothing more than “I want this feeling to stop.”\n\nLater, it can become larger.\n\nI want relationships that feel safe.\n\nI want meaningful work.\n\nI want ordinary mornings.\n\nI want to laugh again.\n\nI want to help someone else.\n\nThe future becomes more than the absence of pain.\n\nIt becomes a life worth protecting.\n\nThat is why small recovery actions matter.\n\nThey are not merely ways to survive today.\n\nThey can be ways of building tomorrow.\n\nA person who learns to recognize warning signs is protecting their future self.\n\nA person who builds supportive relationships is protecting their future self.\n\nA person who learns to ask for help is protecting their future self.\n\nA person who begins again after a setback is protecting their future self.\n\nThere is no single moment when a person becomes finished with recovery.\n\nThere are chapters.\n\nTransitions.\n\nNew challenges.\n\nNew skills.\n\nNew forms of hope.\n\nThe journey continues.\n\nAnd that can be good news.\n\nBecause if recovery were a single moment, one setback could take it away.\n\nBut a journey gives you another step.\n\nThen another.\n\nAnd another.\n\nYou do not need to know the entire route tonight.\n\nYou only need enough light to see the next safe step.',
          cliffhanger: 'The next step is waiting somewhere ahead. You do not have to see the whole road to take it.',
        ),
      ],
    ),

    StoryModel(
      id: 'featured_samhsa_connection',
      authorUid: 'trusted-featured',
      authorName: 'SAMHSA RecoverMe',
      title: 'Connection Can Be Part of Recovery',
      content: 'There is a particular kind of loneliness that makes a room feel smaller.\n\nThe walls do not actually move.\n\nBut the world seems farther away.\n\nA person may have contacts in a phone, followers online, classmates, coworkers, relatives—and still feel as though nobody could understand what is happening inside.\n\nThat feeling can create a dangerous habit.\n\nDisappear until you feel better.\n\nThe problem is that disappearing can make the feeling stronger.\n\nThe quiet room gives every negative thought more space.\n\nConnection offers another possibility.\n\nNot necessarily a crowd.\n\nOne safe person.\n\nOne honest conversation.\n\nOne supportive community.\n\nOne place where you can say, “I am struggling,” without having to perform.\n\nThe first step toward connection can feel surprisingly difficult.\n\nYou may not know what to say.\n\nYou may worry about being a burden.\n\nYou may fear rejection.\n\nThose fears are real.\n\nBut they are not always predictions.\n\nSometimes the person you are afraid to contact is waiting for an opportunity to help.\n\nSometimes a professional is better equipped to respond.\n\nSometimes a peer community provides the understanding you could not find elsewhere.\n\nThe important thing is to create a door.\n\nEven a small one.',
      category: 'Life',
      coverEmoji: '🌍',
      isFeatured: true,
      sourceName: 'SAMHSA RecoverMe',
      sourceUrl: 'https://www.samhsa.gov/recoverme',
      createdAt: DateTime(2026, 1, 1),
      tags: [
        'connection',
        'support',
        'hope',
        'community',
        'recovery',
      ],
      chapters: [
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 1 — The Quiet Room',
          content: 'There is a particular kind of loneliness that makes a room feel smaller.\n\nThe walls do not actually move.\n\nBut the world seems farther away.\n\nA person may have contacts in a phone, followers online, classmates, coworkers, relatives—and still feel as though nobody could understand what is happening inside.\n\nThat feeling can create a dangerous habit.\n\nDisappear until you feel better.\n\nThe problem is that disappearing can make the feeling stronger.\n\nThe quiet room gives every negative thought more space.\n\nConnection offers another possibility.\n\nNot necessarily a crowd.\n\nOne safe person.\n\nOne honest conversation.\n\nOne supportive community.\n\nOne place where you can say, “I am struggling,” without having to perform.\n\nThe first step toward connection can feel surprisingly difficult.\n\nYou may not know what to say.\n\nYou may worry about being a burden.\n\nYou may fear rejection.\n\nThose fears are real.\n\nBut they are not always predictions.\n\nSometimes the person you are afraid to contact is waiting for an opportunity to help.\n\nSometimes a professional is better equipped to respond.\n\nSometimes a peer community provides the understanding you could not find elsewhere.\n\nThe important thing is to create a door.\n\nEven a small one.',
          cliffhanger: 'The door was small—but someone on the other side was about to answer.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 2 — One Honest Message',
          content: 'The message did not need to be dramatic.\n\n“Can we talk?”\n\nThat was enough.\n\nThe reply came later than expected.\n\nFor a moment, disappointment returned.\n\nThen the phone vibrated.\n\n“I’m here.”\n\nTwo words.\n\nThey did not solve the problem.\n\nBut they interrupted the belief that nobody cared.\n\nThe conversation was messy.\n\nThere were long pauses.\n\nSome sentences were unfinished.\n\nThat was okay.\n\nConnection does not require perfect communication.\n\nIt requires enough safety to keep trying.\n\nOver time, the person began learning what kind of support helped.\n\nSometimes listening.\n\nSometimes distraction.\n\nSometimes practical help.\n\nSometimes encouragement to seek professional care.\n\nSometimes just company.\n\nThis mattered because “I need help” is not one single request.\n\nSupport becomes more useful when you can describe what kind.\n\nThe relationship also became more honest.\n\nThere were boundaries.\n\nThere were moments when the other person could not respond immediately.\n\nThere were misunderstandings.\n\nBut instead of treating every disappointment as abandonment, they learned to repair.\n\nThat was another part of connection.\n\nNot perfection.\n\nRepair.\n\nThe quiet room began to feel different.\n\nNot because it had disappeared.\n\nBecause it no longer felt like the only room available.',
          cliffhanger: 'But then the message that usually arrived every evening did not come—and the old loneliness returned.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 3 — When People Cannot Always Be There',
          content: 'The unanswered message hurt.\n\nThe mind quickly created an explanation.\n\nThey do not care.\n\nI am too much.\n\nI should stop reaching out.\n\nBut reality was less dramatic.\n\nThe person was simply unavailable.\n\nThat distinction mattered.\n\nConnection cannot mean having constant access to another human being.\n\nPeople have their own lives.\n\nTheir own limits.\n\nTheir own difficult days.\n\nHealthy connection includes those limits.\n\nSo the person tried something new.\n\nInstead of depending on one door, they remembered the other doors.\n\nA family member.\n\nA friend.\n\nA professional.\n\nA peer space.\n\nA routine that helped create stability.\n\nThe network did not have to be huge.\n\nIt only had to be real.\n\nThe loneliness did not disappear instantly.\n\nBut it became manageable.\n\nThis was a powerful lesson.\n\nSupport is stronger when it is distributed.\n\nOne person can care deeply and still not be available.\n\nA community can care and still have boundaries.\n\nProfessional support can provide something friends cannot.\n\nDifferent kinds of connection can work together.\n\nThat night, the person stepped out of the room.\n\nThe world outside was ordinary.\n\nTraffic.\n\nLights.\n\nPeople going somewhere.\n\nFor a few minutes, being part of the world felt enough.\n\nThen came a thought:\n\nMaybe connection is not only something I receive.\n\nMaybe it is also something I can help create.',
          cliffhanger: 'The next day, one small act of connection would turn the person from someone waiting for support into someone offering it.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 4 — Becoming Part of the Circle',
          content: 'The act was simple.\n\nSomeone else looked distant.\n\nThe person sent a message.\n\n“Hey. You okay?”\n\nNo pressure.\n\nNo demand for an explanation.\n\nJust an open door.\n\nThe response did not arrive immediately.\n\nBut the act mattered.\n\nConnection had become a practice.\n\nYou reach.\n\nYou listen.\n\nYou respect boundaries.\n\nYou ask.\n\nYou allow people to say no.\n\nYou ask for help when you need it.\n\nYou encourage professional support when the problem is bigger than a friendship can hold.\n\nYou keep building.\n\nOver time, the person understood that recovery is not about becoming completely independent from everyone else.\n\nHumans are social.\n\nSupport can be part of resilience.\n\nA meaningful life often contains relationships, purpose, community, and belonging.\n\nConnection does not remove every painful emotion.\n\nIt can make those emotions less isolating.\n\nThat difference matters.\n\nThe quiet room may still exist.\n\nBut now there are doors.\n\nThere are people.\n\nThere are places to go.\n\nThere are skills to use.\n\nAnd there is the knowledge that one difficult night does not have to become a permanent address.\n\nThe person looked at the phone.\n\nA new message appeared.\n\n“Want to talk?”\n\nThey smiled.\n\nThis time, they answered immediately.\n\n“Yeah.”\n\nAnd the room suddenly felt a little bigger.',
          cliffhanger: 'Sometimes recovery begins with a door. Sometimes it continues because you learn to leave that door open for someone else.',
        ),
      ],
    ),

    StoryModel(
      id: 'featured_amber_luckey_worth_fighting_for',
      authorUid: 'trusted-featured',
      authorName: 'Amber Luckey',
      title: 'I Was Worth Fighting For',
      content: 'There are evenings when a difficult feeling arrives loudly.\n\nAnd then there are evenings when it arrives quietly, sits beside you, and slowly convinces you that nothing you do will make a difference.\n\nThat was the kind of evening Amber knew too well.\n\nThe room was ordinary. The light from the window had faded. Her phone was close enough to reach without standing up, and far enough away to feel like a decision.\n\nShe had spent the day trying to function normally.\n\nThen the thoughts arrived.\n\nYou should be doing better.\n\nWhy is this still hard?\n\nWhat is wrong with you?\n\nThe thoughts did not sound dramatic. They sounded reasonable.\n\nAmber had learned that depression and trauma could make a temporary moment feel permanent.\n\nShe reached for her phone.\n\nFor a moment, she considered disappearing into it.\n\nThen she stopped.\n\nInstead of asking how to fix her entire life tonight, she asked a smaller question:\n\n“What would make the next ten minutes a little safer?”\n\nShe did not have an answer.\n\nThat frightened her.\n\nBut the pause mattered.\n\nNothing magical happened.\n\nThe heaviness remained.\n\nYet there was now a little space between the thought and the action.\n\nThen her phone lit up.\n\nOne message.\n\nOne name.\n\nAmber stared at the screen without opening it.\n\nBecause sometimes the hardest part of accepting help is deciding whether you are ready to let someone see that you need it.',
      category: 'Motivation',
      coverEmoji: '💪',
