      chapters: [
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 1 — The Evening That Would Not End',
          content: 'There are evenings when a difficult feeling arrives loudly.\n\nAnd then there are evenings when it arrives quietly, sits beside you, and slowly convinces you that nothing you do will make a difference.\n\nThat was the kind of evening Amber knew too well.\n\nThe room was ordinary. The light from the window had faded. Her phone was close enough to reach without standing up, and far enough away to feel like a decision.\n\nShe had spent the day trying to function normally.\n\nThen the thoughts arrived.\n\nYou should be doing better.\n\nWhy is this still hard?\n\nWhat is wrong with you?\n\nThe thoughts did not sound dramatic. They sounded reasonable.\n\nAmber had learned that depression and trauma could make a temporary moment feel permanent.\n\nShe reached for her phone.\n\nFor a moment, she considered disappearing into it.\n\nThen she stopped.\n\nInstead of asking how to fix her entire life tonight, she asked a smaller question:\n\n“What would make the next ten minutes a little safer?”\n\nShe did not have an answer.\n\nThat frightened her.\n\nBut the pause mattered.\n\nNothing magical happened.\n\nThe heaviness remained.\n\nYet there was now a little space between the thought and the action.\n\nThen her phone lit up.\n\nOne message.\n\nOne name.\n\nAmber stared at the screen without opening it.\n\nBecause sometimes the hardest part of accepting help is deciding whether you are ready to let someone see that you need it.',
          cliffhanger: 'The message waiting on her phone would force Amber to make a choice she had been avoiding for a very long time.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 2 — The Question Behind the Question',
          content: 'The message was simple.\n\n“How are you really doing?”\n\nAmber knew the easy answer.\n\nFine.\n\nShe typed it.\n\nDeleted it.\n\nTyped, “Just tired.”\n\nDeleted that too.\n\nFinally, she wrote:\n\n“I’m having a hard night.”\n\nShe nearly deleted it.\n\nInstead, she pressed send.\n\nThe reply did not solve anything.\n\nBut it changed the size of the problem.\n\nShe did not need to explain her entire history.\n\nShe did not need to prove that she deserved support.\n\nShe only needed to stop carrying the whole weight by herself.\n\nThat became a pattern.\n\nWhen difficult thoughts appeared, Amber practiced smaller questions.\n\nWhat am I feeling?\n\nWhat happened before this?\n\nWhat do I need right now?\n\nDo I need rest?\n\nSpace?\n\nSomeone to listen?\n\nProfessional support?\n\nSometimes the answer was “I don\'t know.”\n\nThat answer used to feel like failure.\n\nNow she treated it as information.\n\nTherapy became less about finding one magical sentence and more about learning the language of her own mind.\n\nShe noticed triggers.\n\nShe noticed that exhaustion made everything louder.\n\nShe noticed that isolation gave negative thoughts more room to grow.\n\nShe noticed that asking for help earlier changed what happened next.\n\nProgress was not always visible.\n\nSome weeks were good.\n\nSome were messy.\n\nBut there was a difference.\n\nBefore, a difficult day meant, “I’m back at the beginning.”\n\nNow it meant, “Today is difficult.”\n\nOne sentence turned a moment into an identity.\n\nThe other allowed the moment to end.\n\nAmber thought she had finally understood the lesson.\n\nThen came a week when the old symptoms returned with a force she had not expected.',
          cliffhanger: 'This time the question was not whether Amber could survive a difficult evening. It was whether she would mistake a setback for the end of recovery.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 3 — The Week That Looked Like Failure',
          content: 'Sleep became difficult.\n\nConcentration disappeared.\n\nOrdinary responsibilities felt strangely heavy.\n\nAmber recognized the pattern, but recognition did not make it painless.\n\nShe became impatient with herself.\n\n“I should know how to handle this by now.”\n\nThe sentence returned again and again.\n\nBut this time, she caught one word.\n\nShould.\n\nShe changed the sentence.\n\n“I am learning how to handle this.”\n\nInstead of judging the week, she began documenting it.\n\nWhat happened before the symptoms became stronger?\n\nHow much had she slept?\n\nHad she isolated herself?\n\nHad she stopped doing the things that helped?\n\nHad she told anyone?\n\nThe notes were not a perfect explanation.\n\nThey were a map.\n\nA setback could become evidence.\n\nNot evidence that she was broken.\n\nEvidence about what she needed.\n\nShe returned to professional support.\n\nShe asked questions.\n\nShe admitted when something was not working.\n\nShe allowed relationships to become part of recovery instead of treating independence as the only form of strength.\n\nSlowly, the week loosened its grip.\n\nNot because depression disappeared.\n\nBecause Amber stopped demanding that recovery happen perfectly.\n\nNear the end of the week, she looked back at her journal.\n\nThe difficult days were revealing patterns.\n\nAnd patterns could be worked with.\n\nThat night, she closed the journal feeling something quieter than happiness.\n\nTrust.\n\nNot complete trust that tomorrow would be easy.\n\nTrust that if tomorrow was hard, she would have more than one way to respond.\n\nThen another difficult thought appeared.\n\n“What if this is just who you are?”\n\nAmber looked at the sentence.\n\nFor the first time, she did not immediately believe it.',
          cliffhanger: 'The thought was still there—but now Amber had a question of her own, and the answer could change the way she saw herself forever.',
        ),
        StoryChapter(
          title: 'Chapter 4 — A Life Bigger Than the Diagnosis',
          content: 'The next morning was not magical.\n\nAmber woke up tired.\n\nThe past was still the past.\n\nHer mind could still become cruel when she was exhausted or afraid.\n\nBut something had shifted.\n\nShe no longer wanted recovery to mean becoming a completely different person.\n\nShe wanted a life that could contain difficult parts without being controlled by them.\n\nThat meant ordinary things.\n\nWork.\n\nRelationships.\n\nSmall plans.\n\nRest.\n\nConversation.\n\nIt meant allowing herself to have a future while some parts of her were still healing.\n\nHope was not a promise.\n\nIt was permission to continue.\n\nShe began protecting the things that made life meaningful.\n\nShe made room for people who respected her boundaries.\n\nShe became more willing to talk about what she needed.\n\nShe stopped treating support as something she had to earn after becoming “better.”\n\nSupport was part of getting better.\n\nA bad morning did not erase a good month.\n\nA setback did not delete everything she had learned.\n\nA symptom did not become her identity.\n\nA diagnosis did not become the full story of a person.\n\nOne afternoon, she returned to the journal.\n\nThe old sentence was still there:\n\n“What if this is just who you are?”\n\nUnder it, she wrote:\n\n“This is something I live with. It is not everything I am.”\n\nIt was not a dramatic ending.\n\nThere was no final battle.\n\nInstead, there was a decision.\n\nShe would keep asking questions.\n\nKeep learning.\n\nKeep reaching for professional help when needed.\n\nKeep letting trusted people stand beside her.\n\nAnd when her mind said that a difficult day proved she was failing, she would remember the difference between a chapter and an entire book.\n\nThat evening, she went outside.\n\nNothing extraordinary happened.\n\nFor a few minutes, she simply felt present.\n\nShe thought about the person she had been when recovery first felt impossible.\n\nShe wished she could go back and say one thing:\n\n“You are not required to know how the whole story ends.”\n\nThen she noticed the journal had one final blank page.\n\nFor once, she was not afraid of it.',
          cliffhanger: 'The blank page was not an ending. It was an invitation—and Amber finally understood that the next part of her life did not have to be written by her worst days.',
        ),
      ],
    ),
  ];
